#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188002 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/id #uuid "175f3917-8801-515d-a493-5f36a52aa01b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10097" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n${\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 2, y \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_Y(y) = \\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$ for $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$\n2. $f(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the marginal density function for $Y$, we get\n$f_{Y}(y)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}dx; = \\frac{1}{4}e^{-y/2}[\\frac{x^{2}}{2}]_{0}^{2}; = \\frac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}$,\nwhere $y \\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the marginal density function for $X$, we get\n$f_{X}(x)|= \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}\\frac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}dy; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{b}e^{-y/2}dy; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)[- 2e^{-y/2}]_{0}^{b}; = \\frac{1}{4}xlim_(b → ∞)[- 2e^{-b/2} + 2]; = \\frac{x}{2}$,\nwhere $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$. So, when $x \\in \\left[0,2\\right]$ and $y\\in \\left[0,\\infty\\right)$, we obtain that\n$\\underbrace{\\dfrac{1}{4}xe^{-y/2}}_{\\large f(x,y)} = \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_X(x)} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Big(\\dfrac{1}{2}e^{-y/2}\\Big)}_{\\large f_Y(y)}$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188209 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/id #uuid "e4007ddd-a5ed-5f3d-96cc-4c911d97a615" :question/math-academy-id "e-9187" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n${\\frac{2}{9}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$." :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}x \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{18}xy^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y) \\in [0, 3] \\times [0, 2]$. Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{18}xy^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 3, 0 \\le y \\le 2 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188210 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee434eee-2eb0-5a0b-b4a1-1be98bf5b217" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Joint Probability for Some Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/id #uuid "1e1f0ecb-6f45-53ac-ae5d-ff953567aefa" :question/math-academy-id "e-9188" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n${\\frac{10}{3x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind $P(X \\geq 3, Y \\leq 2)$." :question/worked-solution "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y$, where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10}{3x^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{4}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{5y}{6x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n(x,y) \\in \\left[2,5\\right]\\times \\left[1,3\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{5y}{6x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 5, 1 \\le y \\le 3 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 3, Y \\le 2) &= \\int_{3}^{5}[\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{5y}{6x^{2}}dy]dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6x^{2}}[\\frac{y^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{6x^{2}}(\\frac{3}{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{3}^{5}\\frac{5}{4x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= [- \\frac{5}{4x}]_{3}^{5} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{4}(\\frac{1}{5} - \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188274 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8382e740-f1a5-52f6-97f5-df8c11929ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint PDF of Two Independent Random Variables Using Marginal PDFs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2972b8aa-250f-5bd2-8165-85701bb5ae9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using only the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185557} {:answer/id #uuid "39118d53-c6ac-5e5e-8dbd-9482f02b9097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = {\\frac{3x^{2}}{4y^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185558} {:answer/id #uuid "49679928-dcc8-5c23-99f1-caab5e7e4a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = {12x^{2}y^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185559} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8aefa0-ddb0-52a8-905d-267a28db4fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = {4x^{2}y^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185560} {:answer/id #uuid "7e679ae5-7861-5259-96b5-c73f8e0a498e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x, y) = {\\frac{4y^{2}}{3x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185561}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8245d5cc-a0d3-5155-a8be-2a54d7d8e0a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185556}] :question/id #uuid "b3e96d9d-5e09-5192-9419-9c5668ecfb87" :question/math-academy-id "q-136861" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$f_{X}(x) = {3x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwisef_{Y}(y) = {4y^{3},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x, y)$.\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188295 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70824c34-6527-5fe5-a86f-2ca683231f75" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Continuous Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe78ca8-334b-531b-a512-a341ac492a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185563} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be7868-6891-5064-82ca-d0abcd776c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185564} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbd88c7-a64d-54bd-9593-2d1a47d427e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185565} {:answer/id #uuid "2262bddb-2ca0-514c-9db6-75b84a2dfe4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185566} {:answer/id #uuid "7afee5a8-f42b-5860-a3d2-673f5458e48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185567}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b9a62c9a-789e-53e7-a2b4-febcf8bd0baf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185562}] :question/id #uuid "e49ff8a6-6b90-594a-be32-eb49510ed619" :question/math-academy-id "q-136864" :question/problem "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$f(x, y) = {\\frac{3}{2}(x^{2} + y^{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f_{X}(x) = \\frac{3}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}$ for $x ∈ [0, 1]$\n2. $f(x, y) = f_{X}(x) \\cdot f_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188563 :